2. If AutoCAD agrees with BricsCAD: make clautolisp's document-namespace
   behaviour the vendors' (an error with an empty message for both
   functions), keeping the message / value only where a VLX boundary
   exists. If the vendors differ: discriminate by dialect per the Dialect
   Divergence rules, with a portability warning under --strict.
3. Record the measurement in the autolisp-spec entries' *** clautolisp
   sections (VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR, VL-EXIT-WITH-VALUE).

* AutoCAD measured (2026-10-23) -- DONE, 2.3.38

control-flow probe on MR !471, every case inside vl-catch-all-apply:

| case                               | AutoCAD 2022 GUI | accoreconsole | BricsCAD V25 Win | BricsCAD V26 mac |
|------------------------------------+------------------+---------------+------------------+------------------|
| (vl-exit-with-error "boom")        | ERROR nil        | ERROR nil     | ERROR ""         | ERROR ""         |
| (vl-exit-with-value 42)            | ERROR nil        | ERROR nil     | ERROR ""         | ERROR ""         |
| ... inside a defun (both)          | ERROR nil        | ERROR nil     | ERROR ""         | ERROR ""         |

(jobs 17398575920, 17398575921, 17398575919, 17396028047.)

So outside a separate-namespace VLX both functions are an error WITHOUT a
message on every product; the products differ only in the empty value
(nil vs ""). clautolisp loads no VLX, so 2.3.38 makes every call that:
VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR / -VALUE raise the AutoLISP error :VL-EXIT-OUTSIDE-VLX
(message "") at the call site; vl-catch-all-apply's catch-all carries
message nil except under the BricsCAD dialects ("") -- the catch-all's
message slot now admits nil, and VL-CATCH-ALL-ERROR-MESSAGE returns nil for
it. *error* (local or global) is called with "" -- what a CAD passes its
*error* in this case is not measured (alfe cannot reach the CAD's top-level
*error*). Tests: builtin-tests (4 rewritten), autolisp-test
tests/error/vl-exit.lsp; spec *** clautolisp sections updated.
